\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 \{\uc0\u8232 "question_id": "Q04",\u8232 "marks": [\u8232 \{\u8232 "no": 1,\u8232 "mark": "Partly",\u8232 "evidence": "merely because the nature of the dispute is primarily of a civil nature, the criminal prosecution cannot be quashed because in cases of forgery and fraud",\u8232 "reason": "It explains that a civil character does not by itself bar criminal proceedings involving forgery, but does not directly address ownership disputes being given criminal colour.",\u8232 "confidence": "high"\u8232 \},\u8232 \{\u8232 "no": 2,\u8232 "mark": "Partly",\u8232 "evidence": "It is admitted that two civil suits are also pending between the parties.",\u8232 "reason": "It describes a property dispute involving alleged forged documents and pending civil suits, but does not state the governing rule or holding.",\u8232 "confidence": "high"\u8232 \},\u8232 \{\u8232 "no": 3,\u8232 "mark": "Misleading",\u8232 "evidence": "when the question regarding validity of a document is subjudice in the civil courts, criminal prosecution, on the allegation of the document being forged, cannot be instituted.",\u8232 "reason": "It quotes the rejected High Court view, while the passage itself says the High Court was not justified in quashing the proceedings.",\u8232 "confidence": "high"\u8232 \},\u8232 \{\u8232 "no": 4,\u8232 "mark": "Yes",\u8232 "evidence": "The allegation that forged and fabricated documents are used by the appellant can also be dealt with in the said suit.",\u8232 "reason": "It directly holds that ownership-related civil issues and alleged forged documents could be dealt with in the civil suit and criminal continuation was abusive.",\u8232 "confidence": "high"\u8232 \},\u8232 \{\u8232 "no": 5,\u8232 "mark": "Partly",\u8232 "evidence": "the dispute was of a civil nature and with an oblique motive it was given a colour of criminal litigation.",\u8232 "reason": "It directly identifies the claimed criminal colouring of a civil dispute, but merely records a party's position without stating the court's holding.",\u8232 "confidence": "high"\u8232 \},\u8232 \{\u8232 "no": 6,\u8232 "mark": "Partly",\u8232 "evidence": "Accepting such a general proposition would be against the provisions of law inasmuch as in all cases of cheating and fraud, in the whole transaction, there is generally some element of civil nature.",\u8232 "reason": "It directly addresses civil disputes and alleged forgery but holds that civil proceedings do not automatically preclude criminal proceedings.",\u8232 "confidence": "high"\u8232 \},\u8232 \{\u8232 "no": 7,\u8232 "mark": "Partly",\u8232 "evidence": "The proceedings could not be quashed only because the respondents had filed a civil suit with respect to the aforesaid documents.",\u8232 "reason": "It concerns alleged forged documents alongside civil litigation, but its rule is that the civil suit alone does not justify quashing criminal proceedings.",\u8232 "confidence": "high"\u8232 \},\u8232 \{\u8232 "no": 8,\u8232 "mark": "Partly",\u8232 "evidence": "nor the dispute being of civil nature was a bar to criminal proceedings, if a case was made out.",\u8232 "reason": "It directly addresses civil disputes and alleged forgery, but emphasizes that criminal proceedings remain possible where a criminal case is actually made out.",\u8232 "confidence": "high"\u8232 \},\u8232 \{\u8232 "no": 9,\u8232 "mark": "Partly",\u8232 "evidence": "facts may give rise to a civil claim and also amount to an offence.",\u8232 "reason": "It states the important distinction that civil character does not prevent criminal proceedings, but does not specifically discuss criminal colouring of ownership disputes.",\u8232 "confidence": "high"\u8232 \},\u8232 \{\u8232 "no": 10,\u8232 "mark": "Partly",\u8232 "evidence": "Obtaining signature of a person on blank sheet of papers by itself is not an offence of forgery or the like.",\u8232 "reason": "It discusses when conduct in a civil dispute can constitute forgery, but concerns a truck seizure rather than property ownership.",\u8232 "confidence": "medium"\u8232 \},\u8232 \{\u8232 "no": 11,\u8232 "mark": "Yes",\u8232 "evidence": "The purely civil dispute, is sought to be given a colour of a criminal offence to wreak vengeance against the Appellant.",\u8232 "reason": "It directly states that a purely civil dispute was given criminal colour and characterizes the resulting FIR as an abuse of process.",\u8232 "confidence": "high"\u8232 \},\u8232 \{\u8232 "no": 12,\u8232 "mark": "Misleading",\u8232 "evidence": "It was held by the Hon\'92ble Supreme Court that when the question regarding validity of a document is subjudice in the civil courts, criminal prosecution, on the allegation of the document being forged, cannot be instituted.",\u8232 "reason": "It reproduces the High Court's rejected reasoning and immediately states that the High Court was not justified in quashing the proceedings.",\u8232 "confidence": "high"\u8232 \},\u8232 \{\u8232 "no": 13,\u8232 "mark": "Yes",\u8232 "evidence": "the growing tendency of complainants attempting to give the cloak of a criminal offence to matters which are essentially and purely civil in nature",\u8232 "reason": "It directly recognizes criminal colouring of essentially civil disputes while explaining that genuine criminal ingredients can still support prosecution.",\u8232 "confidence": "high"\u8232 \},\u8232 \{\u8232 "no": 14,\u8232 "mark": "Partly",\u8232 "evidence": "Therefore, execution of such document (purporting to convey some property of which he is not the owner) is not execution of a false document as defined under section 464 of the Code.",\u8232 "reason": "It directly concerns property documents and forgery, but focuses on the technical definition of a false document rather than criminal colouring generally.",\u8232 "confidence": "high"\u8232 \},\u8232 \{\u8232 "no": 15,\u8232 "mark": "Partly",\u8232 "evidence": "The proceedings could not be quashed only because the respondents had filed a civil suit with respect to the aforesaid documents.",\u8232 "reason": "It addresses forged documents in civil litigation but emphasizes that civil proceedings alone do not eliminate criminal liability.",\u8232 "confidence": "high"\u8232 \}\u8232 ],\u8232 "good_answer_present": "Yes",\u8232 "best_passage_no": 13,\u8232 "caveats": "Passages 3 and 12 quote the rejected High Court position that a pending civil challenge to document validity bars criminal prosecution; they should not be read as stating the Supreme Court's holding."\u8232 \}\
